    {
      "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011-sch-2-sec-12cb",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "12CB Unconscionable conduct in connection with financial services",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011#sch-2-sec-12cb"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011.pdf, p.9",
      "values:originalText": "(1) A person must not, in trade or commerce, in connection with:\n\n(a) the supply or possible supply of financial services to a person\n(other than a listed public company); or\n\n(b) the acquisition or possible acquisition of financial services\nfrom a person (other than a listed public company);\nengage in conduct that is, in all the circumstances, unconscionable.\n\n(2) This section does not apply to conduct that is engaged in only\nbecause the person engaging in the conduct:\n\n(a) institutes legal proceedings in relation to the supply or\npossible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible\nacquisition; or\n\n(b) refers to arbitration a dispute or claim in relation to the\nsupply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or\npossible acquisition.\n\n(3) For the purpose of determining whether a person has contravened\nsubsection (1):\n\n(a) the court must not have regard to any circumstances that\nwere not reasonably foreseeable at the time of the alleged\ncontravention; and\n\n(b) the court may have regard to conduct engaged in, or\ncircumstances existing, before the commencement of this\nsection.\n\n(4) It is the intention of the Parliament that:\n\n(a) this section is not limited by the unwritten law of the States\nand Territories relating to unconscionable conduct; and\n\n(b) this section is capable of applying to a system of conduct or\npattern of behaviour, whether or not a particular individual is\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2011A00184\n\nSchedule 2  Unconscionable conduct\n\nidentified as having been disadvantaged by the conduct or\nbehaviour; and\n\n(c) in considering whether conduct to which a contract relates is\nunconscionable, a court’s consideration of the contract may\ninclude consideration of:\n\n(i) the terms of the contract; and\n\n(ii) the manner in which and the extent to which the\ncontract is carried out;\n\nand is not limited to consideration of the circumstances\nrelating to formation of the contract.\n\n(5) In this section:\nlisted public company has the same meaning as it has in the\nIncome Tax Assessment Act 1997.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) A person must not, in trade or commerce, in connection with:\n\n(a) the supply or possible supply of financial services to a person\n(other than a listed public company); or\n\n(b) the acquisition or possible acquisition of financial services\nfrom a person (other than a listed public company);\nengage in conduct that is, in all the circumstances, unconscionable.\n\n(2) This section does not apply to conduct that is engaged in only\nbecause the person engaging in the conduct:\n\n(a) institutes legal proceedings in relation to the supply or\npossible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible\nacquisition; or\n\n(b) refers to arbitration a dispute or claim in relation to the\nsupply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or\npossible acquisition.\n\n(3) For the purpose of determining whether a person has contravened\nsubsection (1):\n\n(a) the court must not have regard to any circumstances that\nwere not reasonably foreseeable at the time of the alleged\ncontravention; and\n\n(b) the court may have regard to conduct engaged in, or\ncircumstances existing, before the commencement of this\nsection.\n\n(4) It is the intention of the Parliament that:\n\n(a) this section is not limited by the unwritten law of the States\nand Territories relating to unconscionable conduct; and\n\n(b) this section is capable of applying to a system of conduct or\npattern of behaviour, whether or not a particular individual is\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2011A00184\n\nSchedule 2  Unconscionable conduct\n\nidentified as having been disadvantaged by the conduct or\nbehaviour; and\n\n(c) in considering whether conduct to which a contract relates is\nunconscionable, a court’s consideration of the contract may\ninclude consideration of:\n\n(i) the terms of the contract; and\n\n(ii) the manner in which and the extent to which the\ncontract is carried out;\n\nand is not limited to consideration of the circumstances\nrelating to formation of the contract.\n\n(5) In this section:\nlisted public company has the same meaning as i",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011-sch-2-sec-12cb-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011-sch-2-sec-12cb-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011-sch-2-sec-12cb-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011-sch-2-sec-12cb-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011-sch-2-sec-12cb-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "3be4a8eb96e154fbf6a3d139ad922570b4878d2d7c624f1874ade8f6e095ac5c"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/competition-and-consumer-legislation-amendment-act-2011#sch-2-sec-12cb",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "12CB Unconscionable conduct in connection with financial services",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 9,
      "values:originalText": "(1) A person must not, in trade or commerce, in connection with:\n\n(a) the supply or possible supply of financial services to a person\n(other than a listed public company); or\n\n(b) the acquisition or possible acquisition of financial services\nfrom a person (other than a listed public company);\nengage in conduct that is, in all the circumstances, unconscionable.\n\n(2) This section does not apply to conduct that is engaged in only\nbecause the person engaging in the conduct:\n\n(a) institutes legal proceedings in relation to the supply or\npossible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or possible\nacquisition; or\n\n(b) refers to arbitration a dispute or claim in relation to the\nsupply or possible supply, or in relation to the acquisition or\npossible acquisition.\n\n(3) For the purpose of determining whether a person has contravened\nsubsection (1):\n\n(a) the court must not have regard to any circumstances that\nwere not reasonably foreseeable at the time of the alleged\ncontravention; and\n\n(b) the court may have regard to conduct engaged in, or\ncircumstances existing, before the commencement of this\nsection.\n\n(4) It is the intention of the Parliament that:\n\n(a) this section is not limited by the unwritten law of the States\nand Territories relating to unconscionable conduct; and\n\n(b) this section is capable of applying to a system of conduct or\npattern of behaviour, whether or not a particular individual is\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2011A00184\n\nSchedule 2  Unconscionable conduct\n\nidentified as having been disadvantaged by the conduct or\nbehaviour; and\n\n(c) in considering whether conduct to which a contract relates is\nunconscionable, a court’s consideration of the contract may\ninclude consideration of:\n\n(i) the terms of the contract; and\n\n(ii) the manner in which and the extent to which the\ncontract is carried out;\n\nand is not limited to consideration of the circumstances\nrelating to formation of the contract.\n\n(5) In this section:\nlisted public company has the same meaning as it has in the\nIncome Tax Assessment Act 1997."
    },
